.\" Manual page for SKSHOW
.\"
.\" FACILITY:  SKSHOW - a nano-utility to show TCP/IP socket counters
.\" MODULE:    doc/skshow.8
.\" AUTHOR:    StarLet Squad and Ruslan R. Laishev (AKA: BadAss SysMan)
.\"
.TH SKSHOW 8 "11 September 2026" "SKSHOW X-03-00" "System Administration Utilities"
.
.SH NAME
skshow \- display the TCP/IP sockets of the system with their counters
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B skshow
.BR SHOW " [" \fIqualifier\fR ...]
.br
.B skshow
.BR MONITOR " [" \fIqualifier\fR ...]
.br
.B skshow
.BR HELP " [" \fItopic\fR " ...] [" /PAGE " | " /NOPAGE ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B skshow
lists the TCP and UDP sockets of the system together with the number of octets
and segments each has carried, and can select among them by address, port,
protocol, address family, socket state and the name of the owning process.
.PP
The inventory is read from
.IR /proc/net/tcp ", " /proc/net/tcp6 ", " /proc/net/udp " and " /proc/net/udp6 ,
which are always present.  The counters are asked of
.B NETLINK_SOCK_DIAG
through the
.B INET_DIAG_INFO
attribute, which is optional: a kernel built without
.B CONFIG_INET_DIAG
merely shows
.B n/a
in the counter columns, and everything else is displayed as usual.  UDP has no
per socket octet counters and its rows carry
.B n/a
as well.
.PP
A verb and a qualifier may be abbreviated as long as the abbreviation stays
unambiguous, and a qualifier is introduced by either a
.B /
or a
.BR -- .
A lone
.BI - name
is not a qualifier: the command line service rejects it as an unrecognized
token, so a selection written that way is refused rather than quietly dropped.
A qualifier that takes a list accepts either one value or a parenthesised list,
and a record matches when it matches any element of it.
.
.SH VERBS
.TP
.B SHOW
Display one snapshot of the socket table and exit.
.TP
.B MONITOR
Redraw the socket table every
.B /INTERVAL
seconds until interrupted, or until
.B /COUNT
cycles have been performed.
.TP
.BI HELP " topic ..."
Display the on-line help.  Without a topic, the description of the utility and
the list of what may be asked for; with one, that topic alone: a verb, a
qualifier with or without its slash,
.BR EXAMPLES ", " MESSAGES ,
or a message of the utility as a subtopic of
.BR MESSAGES .
The case of the letters does not matter.  The same text is reached by
.BR -h ", " --help ", " /HELP " and " /?
wherever they stand on the command line, and by running the image with no
argument at all.
.IP
The text is read out of the help library of the product,
.IR skshow.hlb ,
installed under
.I share/help
beside the image, or the Russian
.I ru/skshow_ru.hlb
when the locale asks for it; the
.BR vhelp (1)
browser reads the same library.  When no library can be found, a shorter
summary rendered out of the grammar tables of the parser answers instead, so
the help never depends on anything being installed.
.IP
At a terminal the text stops at the end of every page;
.B /NOPAGE
lets it run through, which is what a pipe wants.
.
.SH QUALIFIERS
.SS Selection
.TP
.BI /LIA= address\fR, \fB/RIA=\fIaddress
Select by the local, respectively the remote, IP address.  An IPv4 or an IPv6
literal; the family it belongs to must not contradict
.BR /FAMILY .
.TP
.BI /LPN= port\fR, \fB/RPN=\fIport
Select by the local, respectively the remote, port number, 1 to 65535.
.TP
.BI /PROTO= keyword
.BR TCP ", " UDP " or " ALL .
Default
.BR ALL .
.TP
.BI /FAMILY= keyword
.BR INET ", " INET6 ", " IPV4 ", " IPV6 " or " ALL .
Default
.BR ALL .
.TP
.BI /STATE= keyword
One of
.BR ESTAB ", " SYN_SENT ", " SYN_RECV ", " FIN_WAIT1 ", " FIN_WAIT2 ", "
.BR TIME_WAIT ", " CLOSE ", " UNCONN ", " CLOSE_WAIT ", " LAST_ACK ", "
.BR LISTEN ", " CLOSING " or " ALL ,
or a parenthesised list of them.
.TP
.BI /PMASK= mask
Select by the name of the owning process, or by a parenthesised list of masks.
The case of the letters is ignored.  A mask without a wildcard is a substring
and matches anywhere in the name; a mask carrying a
.BR * ", " % " or " ?
is a pattern that has to match the whole name, where
.B *
stands for any run of characters and
.BR % " and " ?
for exactly one.  Quote a mask the shell would expand.  At most eight masks are
taken.
.
.SS Operation
.TP
.BR /HIGHLIGHT ", " /NOHIGHLIGHT
Show in reverse video a row that has moved since the previous refresh: a
counter, a queue length or the state has changed, or the socket was not there a
cycle ago.  On by default, and marks nothing on the first refresh, when there is
no previous state to differ from.  Escape sequences are emitted only to a
terminal, so a redirected output carries none of this whatever the setting.
.TP
.BR /COUNTERS ", " /NOCOUNTERS
Ask NETLINK for the byte and packet counters.  On by default;
.B /NOCOUNTERS
leaves NETLINK alone, which is the cheaper way to look at a system carrying a
great many sockets.
.TP
.BI /INTERVAL= seconds
The refresh interval of
.BR MONITOR ,
default 3.  Zero turns
.B MONITOR
into a single snapshot.
.TP
.BI /COUNT= number
The number of refresh cycles of
.BR MONITOR ,
default 0 for unlimited.
.
.SS Diagnostics
.TP
.BR /TRACE ", " /NOTRACE
Produce an extensible diagnostic output.  Off by default.
.TP
.BI /LOGFILE= file
A file to accept the diagnostic output and the messages of the utility, which
otherwise go to the standard error.  The socket table is never written to it:
it goes to the standard output whatever the log destination is.
.TP
.BI /LOGSIZE= octets
Rewind the log file once it has grown past this size.  Default 0, no limit.
.
.SS Help
.TP
.BR /PAGE ", " /NOPAGE
Whether the text of
.B HELP
stops at the end of every page.  Paged at a terminal by default, never
elsewhere.
.
.SH OUTPUT
One row per socket: the protocol and family, the local and the remote
end-point, the socket state, the PID and name of the owning process, the
receive and transmit queue lengths, and the octets and segments received and
sent.  A footer names the number of rows displayed and the selection criteria
in effect.
.PP
Escape sequences are emitted only when the standard output is a terminal, so
the output may be piped or redirected without them.
.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A message of the utility is written to the standard error, or to the log file
when
.B /LOGFILE
is given, in the form
.PP
.RS
.nf
%SKSHOW-E-IVADDR, 10.0.0 is not an IPv4 or an IPv6 address, qualifier /LIA
.fi
.RE
.PP
where the letter is the severity and the word after it the name of the message.
.B skshow help messages
lists them, and
.B skshow help messages
.I name
says what to do about one.  The command line service adds messages of its own
about a verb, a qualifier or a value it does not accept.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command was carried out, or the help was displayed.
.TP
.B 1
The command line was rejected, or the run failed.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Listening TCP sockets:
.PP
.RS
.nf
skshow show /state=listen /proto=tcp
.fi
.RE
.PP
Everything a pair of servers is holding on the loopback:
.PP
.RS
.nf
skshow show /lia=127.0.0.1 /pmask=(nginx,redis)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ten refreshes, two seconds apart, without asking NETLINK:
.PP
.RS
.nf
skshow monitor /interval=2 /count=10 /nocounters
.fi
.RE
.PP
Watch a busy host, with the moving rows standing out:
.PP
.RS
.nf
skshow monitor /interval=1
.fi
.RE
.PP
IPv6 sockets that are established or waiting out their close:
.PP
.RS
.nf
skshow monitor /family=inet6 /state=(estab,time_wait)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every SSH-related socket, by a pattern, whatever the case of the name:
.PP
.RS
.nf
skshow show '/pmask=SSH*'
.fi
.RE
.PP
The description of a qualifier:
.PP
.RS
.nf
skshow help pmask
.fi
.RE
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /proc/net/tcp ", " /proc/net/tcp6 ", " /proc/net/udp ", " /proc/net/udp6
The socket inventory.
.TP
.I /proc/<pid>/fd
Scanned to bind a socket inode to the process holding it.
.TP
.I /proc/<pid>/comm
The name of that process.
.TP
.IR <prefix>/share/help/skshow.hlb ", " <prefix>/share/help/ru/skshow_ru.hlb
The help libraries, English and Russian.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SKSHOW_HELPLIB
Names a help library to read instead of the installed one.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_MESSAGES ", " LANG
The first one set decides the language of the help: a value beginning with
.B ru
selects the Russian library.
.
.SH NOTES
The file descriptor directory of a foreign process is readable by the
superuser only.  Run without that privilege,
.B skshow
shows such sockets as
.B ???
and says in the footer how many processes it could not read.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ss (8),
.BR netstat (8),
.BR proc (5),
.BR vhelp (1)
.
